## ABSTRACT
The catalytic conversion of carbon dioxide $(\mathsf{C O}_{2})$ into high value-added chemicals is of great significance to address the pressing carbon  cycle  issues.  Reticular  chemistry  of  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)-based  materials  exhibits  great  potential  and effectiveness to face ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ challenge from capture to conversion. To date, the integrated nanocomposites of nanostructure and MOF  have  emerged  as  a  powerful  heterogeneous  catalysts  featured  with  multifold  advantages  including  synergistic  effects between the two interfaces, confinement effect of meso- and micropores, tandem reaction triggered by multiple active sites, high stability and dispersion, and so on. Given burgeoning carbon cycle and nanostructure $@$ MOFs,  this  review  highlights  some  of important  advancements  to  provide  a  full  understanding  on  the  synthesis  and  design  of  nanostructure@MOFs  composites  to facilitate carbon cycle through ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photocatalytic, electrocatalytic, and thermal conversion. Afterward, the catalytic applications of  some  representative  nanostructure $@$ MOFs  composites  are  categorized,  in  which  the  origin  of  activity  or  structure-activity relationship  is  summarized.  Finally,  the  opportunities  and  challenges  are  proposed  for  inspiring  the  future  development  of nanostructure $@$ MOFs composites for carbon cycle.
## KEYWORDS
nanostructure@MOFs, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ reduction, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ photocatalysis, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ electrocatalysis, ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ thermal catalysis
## Introduction
Excessive  emission  of  carbon  dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)$ has  resulted  in increasingly  serious  global  climatic  problem.  Extensive  endeavor has been devoted in different each stages in the carbon cycle from $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture,  storage  and  conversion.  Thereinto,  direct  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  into  high  value-added  chemicals  through photocatalysis,  electrocatalysis,  thermal  catalysis  and  a  direct combination of the three catalysis has exhibited great promise to mitigate  the  pressing  carbon  cycle  problem  [1, 2].  Moreover, various $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion products from $\mathrm{C}_{1}$ , $C_{2}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}$ to ${\mathrm{C}}_{5}$ molecules can  be  tuned  through  the  transfer  of  multiple  protons  and electrons  to  meet  the  feedstock  requirements  of  different application scenarios [3–8]. However, the intractable challenge in catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion is the high bond enthalpy $+805\ \mathrm{kJ\cdotmol^{-1}}$ in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, signifying that a high energy input is required to break the highly stable $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C=O}$ bond [9–13].
Metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs)  have  been  extensively studied  as  a  powerful  porous  materials  to  address  the  global energy  and  environmental  issues  due  to  the  reticular  chemistry induced the significant structural designability and functionality in MOFs  [1, 2, 14].  Taking  advantage  of  the  free,  abundant  and renewable  energy,  the  photocatalytic  and  electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ have  been  continuously  appealing.  The  exploitation  of  efficient MOFs  photocatalysts  to  achieve  high  solar  light  utilization,  the separation and transfer of photogenerated charge carriers, and the adsorption  and  activation $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules  for  initiating  the reaction is highly attractive [9, 10, 15, 16]. Despite the developments in this field, the efficiency of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction in pure MOF systems is still not satisfactory. Moreover, manipulating the photocatalytic  selectivity  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  to  high  value-added chemicals  is  still  highly  challenging.  As  for  electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  reaction $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ ,  metal-organic  frameworks  (MOFs) have  been  extensively  studied  as  brilliant  electrocatalysts.  Their highly  tunable  structure/active  site/pore  size  and  remarkable specific surface area are beneficial to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule enrichment for promoting $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ activity [17–20]. And the well-defined catalytic environments  in  MOFs  were  also  considered  to  be  a  suitable system  for  catalytic  mechanism  explanation.  The  highly  tunable secondary  building  units  of  metal  clusters  and  organic  linkers enable MOFs to be a flexible platform for the design of complex catalyst.  However,  considering  the  multi-electron  and/or  proton involved $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ process, the poor electrical conductivity and the weak  electron-donating  capability  extremely  constrain  the accomplishment of MOFs being as efficient $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalysts.  Integrating  MOFs  with  electron-rich  and  highconductivity  nanostructure  with  definite  active  site  to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ product  will  probably  develop  into  an  effective  strategy  for enhancing the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ selectivity and efficiency [10, 17, 18, 21–24]. As for the thermal catalysis of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, the pure MOFs have  been  rarely  reported  due  to  the  lack  of  active  site  and  the structure  stability  problem  [25, 26].  But  the  great  porosity  and structural adjustability of MOFs can not only trigger strong metalsupport  interactions  (SMSIs)  between  the  active  nanostructures and the orthogonal metal-coordinating sites from the ligands and metal-oxo cluster secondary building units (SBUs) of MOFs, but also emerge the confinement effect by the nanochannels of MOFs [10, 23, 27, 28]. Although several reviews about MOF and MOFbased  materials  have  been  published  previously,  an  updated review  that  focused  on  nanostructures@MOF  materials  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion is highly necessary considering the rapid development of nanostructure@MOFs materials.
In this review, we systematically summarize the recent advances of  nanostructure@MOFs  materials  at  the  atomic  and  molecular level for heterogeneous $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ catalytic conversion (Fig. 1). First, the synthesis  philosophy  of  nanostructure@MOFs  is  presented. Second,  the  interfacial  interaction  paradigms  of  nanostructure $@$ MOFs  are  described.  Third,  the  applications  of  nanostructure@MOFs  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  are  fully  discussed including  photocatalysis  (Semiconductor/Metal/Photosensitizer/ Quantum  dots/Graphene/POMs/MOF@MOFs),  electrocatalysis (Metal/  Metal  oxide/  POM/  Nanocarbon/  Metallocene@MOFs), photoelectrocatalysis, and thermal catalysis (Metal/Metal oxid/Cluster/Molecular Complex@MOFs). Fourth, elucidation of structure-activity  relationship  in  nanostructure@MOFs  for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  is  demonstrated.  Challenges  and outlooks are discussed at the end of the article aiming at inspiring the rational design of more creative nanostructure@MOFs catalyst for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion.
## 2    Synthesis philosophy of nanostructure@MOF
Although  nanostructure@MOF  multifunctional  materials  have recently aroused great interests, the structural dimension between nanoparticles  and  MOF  determines  the  great  challenge  in  the nanoparticle  or  MOF  overgrowth.  Currently,  the  synthesis  of nanostructure@MOF composites can be summarized in four ways [29].  i)  Introducing  the  precursors  of  nanoparticles  into  the prepared MOF crystals through vapor-phase deposition or liquidphase  impregnation  methods.  The  nanoparticle  crystals  within MOFs  will  be  formed  after  the  utilization  of  further heat/radiation/reducing  inducements  (Fig. 2(a)).  In  2005,  the researchers  reported  the  loading  of  highly  porous  coordination polymers  host  lattices  though  organic-metal  chemical  vapor deposition to achieve the synthesis of metal@MOF [30]. In 2008, the  researchers  prepared $\mathrm{cu}$ and $\mathrm{znO}$ nanostructure@MOF-5 nanocatalyst through gas-phase infiltration and by hydrogenolysis or photoassisted thermolysis with the volatile [CpCuL] $\mathrm{(L=PMe}_{3},$ CNtBu)  and $\mathrm{ZnEt}_{2}$ as  precursors.  The  catalytic  activity  of  fresh catalyst  can  reach  about $60\%$ of  the  industrial  reference  at  the same reaction conditions (1 atm and $220^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . Unfortunately, the stability of the catalyst in methanol synthesis is not yet satisfactory, which suffered from structural collapsing and failed performance under several hours’ operation [31]. The confinement growths of two-dimensional  materials  clusters  or  quantum  dots  with  welldefined  atomic  structure  were  also  developed.  The  nickel(II) chloride, $\mathrm{nickel(II)/cobalt(II)/iron(II)}$ chloride sheets were successfully immobilized in MOF through coordinating with the well-designed six chelating bipyridine linkers, strongly signifying that  the  size,  structure  and  spatial  dispersion  of  inorganic  lowdimension materials can be well modulated through manipulating the  local  pore  environment  of  MOF  [32].  ii)  The  most  widely applied  synthetic  methods  for  nanostructure@MOF  composites are based on a ‘one-pot’ approach in which the MOF encapsulation can be derived in the presence of the functional nanoparticles (Fig. 2(b)). As for the hydrothermal synthesis of nanostructure@MOF materials, the amount of PVP, the ratio of ethanol/DMF,  and  the  crystallization  time  have  been  well elucidated in the synthesis of core-shell Pd@IRMOF-3 through a series of control experiments. Normally, PVP is well known as a stabilizer  to  disperse  Pd  nanoparticles.  In  this  experiment,  the coordination  and  hydrophobic  interactions  between  PVP  and IRMOF-3 precursors can provide suitable affinity to grow MOF shell on the surface of Pd nanoparticles. The ratio of mixed solvent can regulate the nucleation and the subsequent growth of IRMOF3  shells,  thus  determining  the  final  core-shell  Pd@IRMOF-3 nanostructures.  The  crystallization  time  was  found  to  be  a  very effective method to modulate the thickness of the IRMOF-3 shell [33].  A  typical  Au@MOF-5 $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ sensor  with  controlled  size  of single Au nanoparticle core and thickness of the MOF-5 shell was prepared by a facile one-pot method, and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules can be selectively detected in the gas mixtures of $\mathrm{N}_{2},$ CO, and $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ [34]. iii)  The  emerging  pseudomorphic  replication  is  conceptually different  with  the  above  two  approaches.  A  core-shell  inorganic particle needs to be prepared in advance in which the core should be the nanoparticle part while the shell materials can play the role of  inorganic  node  feedstock  for  MOF  growth.  The  MOF encapsulation  will  be  formed  by  well  controlling  the  reaction between  the  inorganic  node  and  the  organic  precursor  ligands (Fig. 2(c)).  In  order  to  obtain  the  porous  coordination  polymer (PCP)  crystals  with  higher-order  structure,  the  shaped nanoparticle  cores  should  play  two  roles,  i.e.,  metal  source  and architecture-directing agent for the subsequent spatial organization  or  morphological  replacement  of  PCP  architecture with  nanoparticle  encapsulation  and  hierarchical  porosity  [35]. Moreover,  a  well-defined  nanoparticle-MOF  interface  was prepared  by  replacing  the  dynamic  dissociated  and  weakly adsorbed  capping  agents  such  as  cetyltrimethylammonium surfactants.  The  MOF  shell  can  grow in  situ on  the  surface  of nanoparticles  without  the  trapped  capping  agent.  The  resultant nanostructure@ZIF-8  and  UiO-66-type  MOFs  samples  show uniform  core-shell  structure  featuring  with  specific  facet alignment. The defined interface of one nanostructure@one MOF nanocrystal provides a high selectivity to unsaturated alcohols in aldehyde  catalytic  hydrogenation  reaction,  compared  with  the conventional  preparation  method.  The  strategy  is  universal  to construct  aligned  and  well-defined  interfaces  between  materials with  diverse  structural  dimensions  [36].  iv)  The  MOFs  and nanoparticles can be individually prepared, and the nanostructure@MOFs can be achieved by subsequent mixing of the two components (Fig. 2(d)) [29]. The key challenges for the construction of nanostructure@MOFs  composites  are  summarized  below  [1, 2, 35]. Firstly, the complete encapsulation of nanoparticles within the
MOF framework. Secondly, the approaches that can fully control the size, morphology, interface and composition of nanostructure@MOFs  composites  are  more  appealing.  Thirdly, the  resultant  composites  have  great  ability  to  overcome  the nanoparticle  agglomeration  as  well  as  the  structural  stability problem. Fourthly, the effective manipulation of different kinds of nanoparticles  in  the  MOF  skeleton  space  will  be  of  greater significance for the future multifunctionality of nanostructure@MOFs.
## 3    Interfacial interaction paradigms of nanostructure@MOF
The  regioselectivity  in  nanostructure@MOF  is  suggested  to  be from steric confinement imposed by the porosity of MOF. Density fuctional  theory  (DFT)  models  implied  that  the  diffusion, configuration and interaction types of reaction molecules will be largely changed at the interface between nanoparticle and MOF. The  resultant  modulation  in  free  energy  barrier  will  lead  to  the controlled reaction activity and selectivity. Based on the previous reports,  the  difference  of  the  interaction  way  (intermediate conformation  on  a  pure  metal  surface/direct  nanoparticle-MOF interface/ill-defined  nanoparticle-MOF  interface)  between  the intermediate  and  the  interface  in  nanostructure@MOF  is summarized  in Fig. 3.  The  conformation-selectivity  relationship was  also  well  verified  through  theoretical  calculation  and experimental tests [37, 38]. The configuration of $\mathsf{a},\mathsf{\beta}$ -unsaturated aldehyde on the pure metal surface shows a flat conformation by π- bond interaction, which results in a low selectivity to unsaturated alcohol  (Fig. 3(a)).  By  contrast,  a  tilt  conformation  at  the  welldefined  nanostructure@MOF  interface  through  a  di-σ  bond  of CHO  moiety  produced  high  selectivity  to  unsaturated  alcohol (Fig. 3(b)). The ill-defined interface usually featured with a gap or capping  agents  trapped  at  the  nanostructure@MOF  interface leading  to  a  weak  control  over  the  intermediate  conformation, thereby implying an unpredictable catalytic activity and selectivity (Fig. 3(c))  [37].  The  different  interaction  modes  usually correspond  to  the  differences  in  construction  methods  of nanostructure@MOF interface. As shown in Fig. 3(d), impregnation method is the traditional strategy but can produce a direct  nanostructure  and  MOF  interface.  As  for  the  coating method,  the  capping  agent  stabilized  nanostructures  were presynthesized,  and  the  presence  of  the  capping  agent  was  also helpful  for  MOFs  anchoring.  Unfortunately,  the  interaction between nanostructure and MOF is indirect owing to the trapped capping agent at the interfaces (Fig. 3(e)). Currently, the one-pot method exhibited great potential to generate direct nanostructure and MOF interface because of no introduction of capping agent (Fig. 3(f)). However, the preparation condition in one-pot method needs to be well tuned. Although some progress has been made, the research on the interactions among the reactant/intermediate/production, the direct/indirect nanostructure and MOF interface, and the catalytic activity/selectivity is still exciting.
## 4 Catalytic $\mathbf{CO}_{2}$ conversion
## 4.1    Photocatalysis
Powered  by  the  free  and  abundant  solar  energy,  photocatalytic reduction  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into  high  value-added  chemicals  is  extremely appealing,  in  which  four  principal  aspects,  i.e.,  light-harvesting, separation  and  transfer  of  photo-generated  charge  carriers,  and adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules, and proper energy band structure for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation. Benefiting from the intrinsic high surface areas and porosity as well as tunable structures, MOFs have exhibited some unique abilities for the light harvesting over broad range, the promoted  separation  of  photo-generated  electron-hole  pairs,  the high accessibility of active sites, the proper platform of identifying the  structure-activity  relationship,  and  so  on.  In  addition,  the strong $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture capability can endow the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction active sites with highly concentrated/enriched $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules to promote the  reaction  balance  for  producing  high-value  chemicals.  The photocatalytic  products,  such  as  CO  and  organic  chemicals (HCOOH, $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4})$ , require different redox potentials $-3.50~\mathrm{eV}$ for CO, $-3.42\ \mathrm{eV}$ for HCOOH, $-3.65~\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ and $-3.79\mathrm{eV}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ to initiate the photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Figs.  4(a) and 4(b))  [15, 39].  As  for  CO  production,  the  twoelectron  photoreduction  process  implies  a  relatively  low  kinetic barrier. The productions of HCOOH, $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ need the transfer  of  different  proton  number  and $2/6/8$ electrons, respectively,  which  has  to  overcome  higher  kinetic  barrier. Normally, the highest occupied molecular orbitals (HOMO) and the  lowest  unoccupied  molecular  orbitals  (LUMO)  of  MOF photocatalysts  determined  the  production  selectivity,  which  are mainly manipulated by modulating the organic linker and/or the metal cluster [40]. Unfortunately, the efficiency and the selectivity of pure MOF materials are still very limited. Interestingly, some recent  publications  have  shown  that  integrating  MOF  with semiconductors,  photosensitizers  or  metal  nanoparticles  to construct nanostructure@MOF composites is responsible for the promoted  photocatalysis  efficiency  and  the  high  production selectivity [17, 41−44]. As shown in Fig. 4(c), the roles of MOF in nanostructure@MOF composites can be classified into photocatalysts, cocatalysts and photocatalytic hosts.
## 4.1.1    Semiconductor@MOFs
The  two-electron  process  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  into  CO  has been widely investigated due to the relatively low kinetic barrier. When the Co-ZIF-9/ZIF-8 was integrated with graphitic carbon nitride $(\mathrm{g-C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4})$ ,  the  composite  photocatalyst  can  coordinate efficiently to promote the conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}$ with  visible-light  illumination  under  mild  reaction  condition,  in which  the  Co-ZIF-9  acts  as  a  cocatalyst  to  promote  the concentration of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at the photocatalytic active sites as well as the  separation  of  light-induced  carriers  [45, 46].  As  for  CdS semiconductor  as  a  photocatalyst,  Co-ZIF-9  cocatalyst  can  also promote  the  photoreduction  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  CO.  Moreover,  the  C source in CO production is also verified by using $^{13}\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ isotopic experiment.  The  apparent  quantum  yield  was  calculated  to  be $1.93\%$ under $420\ \mathrm{nm}$ monochromatic irradiation. The uniqueness of $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF-9 should be ascribed to the enhanced electron transfer according  to  the in-situ photoluminescence  and  photocurrent generation tests [47]. The in-situ synthesized composite of Co-ZIF
$9/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ also showed definite activity for photoreduction of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ owing to the promoted charge separation and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activition  [48].  The  electrostatic  self-assembled  carbon  nitride nanosheet@UiO-66  zirconium-based  MOF  showed  a  high photocatalytic activity for the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO under visible light  driven  reduction.  Compared  with  the  bare  carbon  nitride nanosheets,  the  introduction  of  UiO-66  can  endow  the  hybrid with strong abilities for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture. Moreover, the presence of UiO-66 can also transfer the photo-generated electron from to the photoexcited carbon nitride nanosheet to substantially inhibit the recombination  of  electron-hole  pair,  thus  providing  long-lived electrons  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  [49].  When  the  UiO-66  was further modified with light-harvesting $\mathrm{Cu(II)}$ tetra(4- carboxylphenyl)porphyrin  (CuTCPP),  the  resultant  CuTCPP $\subset$ UiO- ${\cdot66}/\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{(CTU/TiO}_{2}^{\cdot}$ ) exhibited an optimal evolution rate of $31.32\upmu\mathrm{molcdotg^{-1}}.\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction into CO, which is about seven times  higher  than  that  of  pure $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ .  The  combination  of efficacious separation of photo-induced carries between MOF and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and  the  enhanced  light  absorption  originating  from chemically implanted Cu-porphyrin should be responsible for the promoted photocatalytic activity [50]. In addition, $\mathrm{Cd}_{0.2}\mathrm{Zn}_{0.8}\mathrm{S@UiO-66-NH}_{2}$ composite  was  also  prepared  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalysts with a relatively low $\mathrm{CH_{3}O H}$ production rate of 6.8 $\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ [51].  Similar  strategies  were  also  found  in $\mathrm{Zn_{2}G e O_{4}/M g-M O F{-}74}$ and other semiconductors/metal nanostructure@MOFs photocatalytic systems for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic conversion [52]. The $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $\mathrm{MOF@TiO}_{2}$ coreshell was used as a proof of concept to demonstrate the effects of semiconductor  and  MOF.  Ultrafast  spectroscopy  indicated  that the  photo-generated  charge  can  effectively  transfer  from photoexcited  inorganic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ to  Cu-MOF,  thus  providing  longlifetime electrons for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction adsorbed in the nonporous MOF  core  [53].  Recently, ${\mathrm{TiO}}_{2}@{\mathrm{MOF}}$ (MIL-101)  was  prepared through  accurately  filling  MOF  with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles  and exhibited  significant  performance  improvements  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction (Figs. 5(a) and $5(\mathrm{b})_{,}^{\cdot}$ ) [42]. $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles play the role of light-absorbing/electron-generating units. The synergy between $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ nanoparticles  and  catalytic  metal  clusters  in  the matrix of MIL-101 facilitates photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. The photocatalyst  named  as $42\%\mathrm{-TiO}_{2}$ -in-MIL-101-Cr- $\mathbf{\cdotNO}_{2}$ that consists of $42\%\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ in a MIL-101 showed a very high apparent quantum efficiency of $11.3\%$ at a wavelength of $350~\mathrm{nm}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic reduction (Fig. 5(c)). The growth of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ units with a precise regulation of spatial positioning in specific pores of MOF is  the  key  to  the  eye-catching  catalytic  performance.  As  an example  of  metal  oxide  semiconductor  encapsulated  in  MOF, $\mathrm{{Cu}_{2}O}$ nanowires $\boldsymbol{@}\mathrm{MOF_{:}}$ ,  i.e., $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ $\mathrm{BTC}{=}1,3,5$ -benzene tricarboxylate) demonstrated an enhanced photocatalytic efficiency  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  to $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ (Fig. 5(d))  [43].  Such MOF can obviously enhance the stability of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ by suppressing the  water  vapor-triggered  corrosion.  In  addition,  the  photogenerated  electron  transfer  from  the  conduction  band  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ nanowires to the LUMO level of $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ is responsible for the photocatalytic  activity  promotion.  In  order  to  promote  selective photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2^{-}}{\mathrm{to-CH}}_{4},$ some  dual-metal  sites  incorporated
MOF  photocatalysts  have  been  developed.  MOF-808-CuNi exhibited  a  high  production  rate  of $158.7~{\upmu\mathrm{mol}}{\cdot}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}{\cdot}{\mathrm{h}}^{-1}$ for $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ production  (Fig. 5(e)).  The  synergy  effect  and  self-adaptive phenomenon  of  dual-metal-site  pairs  play  important  role  in stabilizing the diverse $\mathrm{C}_{1}$ intermediates of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ pathway (Fig. 5(f)). If  MOF  photocatalysts  with  dual-metal  sites  can  combine  the properties of nanoparticles to build a heterogeneous interface, it is expected that the performance will be further improved (Figs. $5(\mathrm{g})$ and $5(\mathrm{h})\backslash$ [44]. Moreover, Co(BDC)-MOF coated CdS composite catalyst demonstrated an enhanced activity for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.  In  this  study,  the  MOF  coating  was  firstly  used  as  a reaction platform for bridging heterogeneous photosensitizer CdS and  molecular  co-catalysts $\mathrm{[Co(bpy)_{3}]^{2+}}$ .  The  charge  transfer mechanism at the interface in the composite system was clarified through  a  series  of  spectroscopic  and  kinetic  characterizations, providing  a  new  idea  for  the  design  of  a  novel  and  efficient photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction system [54]. Recently, the photothermal  catalysis  that  can  couple  photocatalysis  and photothermal  conversion  is  becoming  more  and  more  popular. The  synergy  can  enhance  the  utilization  of  infrared  light  and promote the rate of charge separation and transport of materials, thus greatly improving the efficiency of catalytic reactions. A UiO66  embedded  with  nanoparticles ${\mathrm{Bi}}_{2}{\mathrm{S}}_{3}$ was  prepared  by  bulkdoping. Upon the irradiation of UV–vis–IR light, the ${\mathrm{Bi}_{2}S_{3}}/{\mathrm{UiO}}\mathrm{-}$ 66  demonstrate  a  thermally-assisted  photo-catalytic  behavior  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  into  CO  at  a  high  rate  of $25.60~\upmu\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1}$ ,  in which the MOF can enhance the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption capacity while the high efficiency of photo-thermal conversion should promote rapid  charge  transfer  for  the  separation  of  photo-generated electron-hole  pairs.  The  new  strategy  that  integrates  the photocatalysis  and  photothermal  assisted  effect  into  one nanostructure  will  open  up  new  vistas  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic reduction [55].
## 4.1.2    Metal@MOFs
The  Au  nanostructure@MOF  nanosheet,  i.e.,  pillared  layered porphyrin  paddle-wheel  framework-3  (PPF-3)  nanosheet,  was designed to promote charge transfer as well as mass transport in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  (Fig. 6(a)).  The  organic  chromophores  has small  cross-section  for  optical  absorption,  while  the  presence  of Au  can  enhance  the  light  absorption  ability  by  the  electrostatic interaction with PPF-3. In addition, the plasmon resonance of Au nanoparticle will induce energy transfer towards PPF-3 nanosheet (Fig. 6(b)). In the case of optimal thickness of PPF-3 nanosheets, the  activity  of $\mathrm{Au}@\$ PPF-3  nanosheet  demonstrate  about  4-fold higher than that of pristine PPF-3 nanosheets for photo-catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  into  HCOOH  (Fig. $6(\mathrm{c})_{,}^{\cdot}$ )  [56].  The  effects  of different metal nanoparticles (Pt and Au) on photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion have also compared. The Pt nanoparticle in $\mathrm{Pt}\ @\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ - MIL-125(Ti)  can  trigger  the  hydrogen  spillover  and  lead  to  the formation  of $\mathrm{Ti}^{3+}$ atom  at  the  bridging  oxygen  linked,  which exhibited a promoted activity for formate production. By contrast, the  presence  of  Au  nanoparticle  in $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-125(Ti)  is detrimental  to  the  reaction.  The  promoting  effect  of  hydrogen spillover induced by $\mathrm{Pt}$ nanoparticle was firstly documented [58]. Moreover,  covalently  attached  photoactive  centers $(\mathrm{Re}^{\mathrm{{I}}}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}$ (BPYDC)Cl, $\mathrm{BPYDC}=2,2^{,}$ -bipyridine- $.5,5^{\prime}$ -di-carboxylate) can be quantitatively and precisely controlled within a zirconium MOF, UiO-67  [59].  The  covalent  interaction  between  Re  photocatalyst and  UiO-67  can  prevents  deactivation  induced  by  the dimerization of the molecular catalyst. Especially, when plasma $\mathrm{Ag}$ nanoparticles  are  embedded  in  Re-UiO-67,  the  intensified  nearAg-surface  electric  fields  can  boost  the  spatially  confined  Re photoactive centers, thus leading to a seven-fold promotion in the conversion $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO under visible light (Fig. 6(d)) [57].
## 4.1.3    Photosensitizer@MOFs
The  strategies  of  modifying  the  composition  and  structure  of functional groups and nodes alone has limited improvement on the  light  absorption  for  photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  of  MOFs. Transition-metal ions complexes normally can serve as electrontransport chains due to the existence of multiple metal redox states and  organic  ligands.  When  cooperated  with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activator  and light harvester, the pathway of high-energy intermediates will be avoided and the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction will be accelerated. The transitionmetal ions (Co, Ni, Mn, $\mathrm{Cu},$ etc.) complexes have been reported to function  as  cocatalysts  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  through enhancing  charge-separation  as  well  as  surface  reaction  kinetics. The combination of chemical redox and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -trapping functions in  framework  substances  is  very  attractive.  Ruthenium-based photosensitizer $\scriptstyle{\mathcal{Q}}\mathbf{C}0$ -ZIF-9 exhibited a very high catalytic turnover number  (TON)  of ${\sim}450$ within $2.5\mathrm{~h~}$ under  mild  illumination conditions  for  photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion  to  CO.  In  this system, $\mathrm{Co}$ -ZIF-9  acted  as  a  strong  porous  cocatalyst  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and concentration [60]. The combination of photosensitizer $\mathrm{[Ru(dmb)_{3}]^{2+}}$ $\mathrm{\Delta^{\prime}d m b{=}}4{,}4^{\prime}$ -dimethyl- $^{2,2^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) and  manganese  bipyridine  complex  incorporated $\mathrm{Zr}$ -MOF demonstrated  an  efficient  catalysis  activity  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  to formate under visible-light irradiation. The sample showed a TON of about 110 in $^{18\mathrm{{h}}}$ superior to the homogeneous reference [61]. Moreover, the integration of photosensitizer $([\mathrm{Ru(bpy)_{3}}]\mathrm{Cl_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ with  high  nuclear ${\mathrm{Co}}_{6}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{MOF}}$ also  showed  a  promoted photocatalytic  activity  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  to  CO  through  the electron transfer from photosensitizer to MOF [62].
## 4.1.4    Quantum dots@MOFs
Most recently, lead halide perovskite (LHP) nanocrystals have also been actively pursued as photocatalysts due to their proper energy band structures, high tolerance of defect, long lifetime of photogenerated  carrier,  and  excellent  visible-light  responses  [63, 64]. The  integration  of  LHP  nanocrystals  and  graphene  oxide  or ${\bf g}_{-}$ $\mathrm{C}_{3}\mathrm{N}_{4}$ have been explored for photocatalytic reduction $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ by  facilitated  charge  separation.  However,  the  LHP nanocrystals lack of satisfactory stability, low $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capturing ability and  effective  catalytic  sites,  thus  hindering  further  performance improvement of for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photocatalytic reduction. A new strategy that  encapsulates $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ )  quantum  dots  in  the nanopores of Fe-porphyrin-based MOFs was developed for visiblelight-driven $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction (Fig. 7) [63]. Taking advantages of the protection  of  the  MOF  framework,  the  photocatalyst  exhibited much higher stability even in water-containing reaction condition. In  addition,  the  photogenerated  electrons  can  be  well  separated from the confined $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ quantum dots to the close contact Fe  catalytic  sites,  thus  leading  to  a  remarkably  promoted photocatalytic activity for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction to CO and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . The total yield is as high as $1{,}559\ \upmu\mathrm{mol{\cdot}g^{-1}}$ about 38-fold of that  of  pure  MOF  without  perovskite  quantum  dots.  The perovskite quantum dots@MOFs indicated an effective approach to improve the stability of quantum dots and facilitate the charge separation  efficiency  of  MOF-based  photocatalysts  [64].  The halide perovskite $\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}$ quantum dots@ZIF composite was also prepared  for  photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction  to  CO  and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ through a facile in situ synthesis process of direct growth/coating of  ZIF  on  quantum  dots.  The  moisture  stability, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture ability,  and  photo-generated  charge  separation  efficiency  of composite  photocatalyst  were  simultaneously  largely  improved. The role of Co centers in ZIF-67 is unique catalytic active sites for further  accelerating  charge  separation  and  activating $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules [63].
## 4.1.5    Graphene@MOFs
Ultrathin  MOFs  nanolayers/nanosheets  were  widely  reported  to expose  rich  readily  accessible  active  sites  and  facilitate  electron transfer/mass  transport,  thereby  ensuring  superior  performance for  many  catalytic  applications.  Moreover,  ultrathin  MOFs nanolayers/nanosheets  usually  represent  accurate  atomic  space structure  which  can  be  used  as  an  ideal  model  for  verifying structure-activity  relationships  [65, 66].  However,  the  reports about  the  synthesis  of  ultrathin  MOFs  nanolayers/nanosheetsbased  photocatalyst  were  very  limited  due  to  the  high  surface energy. Coulomb interaction guided 2D/2D $\mathrm{MOF/rGO}$ heterostructures have also been prepared for photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.  The  strong $\pi-\pi$ interaction  at  2D/2D  MOF/rGO  the heterostructure can trigger boosted electrostatic charge transportation/transfer leading to abundant electron density at the isolated $\mathrm{Ni-N_{4}}$ sites  in  2D  MOF.  The  photo-generated  electron was noticeably harvested on the 2D rGO scaffold. As a result, the obtained CO evolution rate is as high as $3.8\times10^{4}\mathrm{\upmumol.h^{-1}{\cdot}g_{f i l m}}^{-1},$ and the selectivity of $91.74\%$ is also desired [66]. A new 2D/2D $\mathrm{MOF/rGO}$ the  heterostructure  was  prepared  through  using  GO nanosheets as both growth template and electron mediator. The MOF layer represented a uniform coating with a thickness of 1.5 nm about three metal coordination layers. The resultant 2D/2D MOF/rGO hybrid catalyst showed a very high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction  activity  featuring  with $3,133\ \mathrm{\mmol{\cdot}g_{\mathrm{\MOL}}^{-1}}$ CO yield  and $95\%$ CO  selectivity.  The  well-exposed  active  sites  in MOF  layer  and  the  highly  efficient  electron  transfer  are responsible for high performance. The above studies about $\scriptstyle2\mathrm{D}/2\mathrm{D}$ $\mathrm{MOF/rGO}$ hybrid  catalysts  will  also  inspire  the  design  of  other types of 2D/2D photocatalyst (Fig. 8) [65].
## 4.1.6    POMs@MOFs
Polyoxometalates (POMs) own metal oxide polyhedra $(\mathrm{MO}_{x},x{=}5,$ 6) as basic structural unit consisted of transition metal cations and polyoxometalate  polyanions,  in  which  transition  metal  M normally has a high valence, such as $\mathbf{W}^{\mathrm{n}}$ , $\mathbf{Mo}^{\mathrm{u}}$ , VV, $\mathrm{Ta}^{\mathrm{v}}$ , and $\mathrm{Nb^{v}}$ . POMs  as  a  class  of  typical  cluster  compound  were  widely investigated for catalytic applications [67–69]. Taking advantages of the superior traits of POMs including the redox capability, welldefined  active  sites,  tunable  light  absorption,  reversible  electrons acceptance and donation, the POMs can be used as photocatalysts, cocatalysts,  photosensitizers,  and  multi-electron  mediator  in  the application of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction [16, 70–75].
Further investigations on these hypotheses will be explored in future  works.  Encapsulating  of  POMs  into  sulfonic  acid functionalized  MIL-101-Cr  is  also  reported  to  promote $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ photoreduction activity [77]. Ti-substituted POMs in POMs@HKUST-1  was  verified  a  role  of  proton  and  electron reservoir  for  the  activity  promotion  in  photocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO [78].
## 4.1.7    MOF@MOF
Very  recently,  a  MOF@MOF  homojunction  (Co-MOF-3 nanostacks) with highly orientated stacked phase and well-defined junction  interface  was  prepared  through  a  hollow $\mathrm{\Au/Ag}$ nanocubes-directed  process  (Fig. 10).  The  two  phases  presented similar composition of cobalt porphyrin. Interestingly, the crystal structure  and  the  energy  level  alignment  are  slightly  different. Moreover, a straightforward observation of light-generated charge separation  at  the  homojunction  interfaces  of  the  two  stacked nanoplates stacks in Co-MOF-3 nanostacks was obtained through the integration of Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM) under proper  light  illumination.  Compared  with  the  single  MOF nanoplates, the Co-MOF-3 nanostacks exhibited about 2.5 times enhanced  photocatalytic  reduction  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to  CO  in  water  with visible-light illumination. The activity can also stabilize at least 22 h.  The  over-whelming  high  selectivity  to  CO  production  is approaching $100\%$ in water media, which surpassed the majority of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction photocatalysts [79].
## 4.2    Electrocatalysis
Electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ is  considered  as  the  most  representative strategy  to  transform $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules  into  high-valued  products, such as CO, HCOOH, $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $,\mathrm{C_{2}H_{5}O H},$ and so on, due to its great advantages  of  easy  processing,  simple  devices,  and  low  energy consumption [9, 10, 23, 80, 81]. Especially, electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ can  be  easily  operated  using  simple  devices  through  converting renewable  intermittent  power,  such  as  solar  and  wind,  etc. Unfortunately,  the  intrinsically  thermodynamic  stability  and  the low  electron  affinity  make  the  conversion  of  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecule require  to  conquer  high  kinetic  barrier  [82].  In  addition,  the competitive $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ evolution reaction tends to decrease the selectivity to aiming products [70]. Thus, the development of suitable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrocatalysts is very important to achieve high $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ efficiency and  selectivity.  In  the  recent  decade,  various  types  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalysts have been developed including metals/oxides/chalcogenides, metal-free materials, and MOFs, etc [9, 71, 83, 84].  By  contrast,  MOFs  exhibit  favorable $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption as well as well-defined structure metrics and catalytic environments  suitable  for  mechanism  investigation.  However, $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalysis  is  multiple  electron-proton  transfer process.  The  low  electrical  conductivity  and  the  weak  electrondonating  capability  suppress  the  development  of  pure  MOFs  as $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalysts.  Because  of  the  product  diversity  of ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}},$ it is also challenging to improve the catalytic selectivity of MOF  to $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products.  Therefore,  integrating  active  component with  MOFs  to  conquer  the  above  issues  should  be  an  attractive method to promote the selectivity and efficiency of ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}}.$
## 4.2.1    Metal@MOFs
For  example,  the  introducing  of  functional  units  like  copper nanoparticle or 1,10-phenanthroline can increase the selectivity of MOFs  to  generate  HCOOH  or  CO.  However,  the  selection  of desired  materials  or  MOFs  and  the  exploration  of  powerful methods to integrate multi-functionality in MOFs for the efficient generation of high-valued products are still challenging and longsought-after.  The  Ag@Al-PMOF  ( $\mathrm{\cdot}[\mathrm{Al}_{2}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{TCPP})]$ ,  TCPP $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ tetrakis(4-carboxyphenyl)porphyrin)  hybrids  were  synthesized with the intimate contact of organic and inorganic components. The  Ag  nanoparticles  are  well  encapsulated  in  the  Al-PMOF frameworks  while  keeping  a  close  contact  with  the  conductive substrate  (Figs.  11(a) and 11(b)).  Ag  nanostructures  are  well recognized as a highly effective electrocatalyst for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction into CO while the competitive side-reaction hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) is also notable. The interface between $\mathrm{Ag}$ and AlPMOF is verified to largely change the surface electronic structure of  Ag  through  electron  transfer  from  Al-PMOF  to  Ag,  thus leading to suppression for HER but a promotion for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ to CO. With the coating of MOF layer, the enhanced mass transfer and  increased  structural  stability  of  Ag  were  also  observed  (Fig. 11(c))  [85].  Very  recently,  Ag@UiO-66  MOF  was  prepared  to demonstrate  the  extra  role  of  MOF  as  chemical  modularity  in electrocatalysis  (Fig. 11(d)).  Non-catalytic  UiO-66  MOF  was assembled on Ag electrode, which served as a porous membrane to  modulate  the  local  chemical  environment  of  active  sites.  The electrocatalytic  activity  and  selectivity  in  Ag@UiO-66  MOF towards  CO  production  were  simultaneously  promoted. Electrochemical analysis results indicated the double roles of MOF membrane as physical and electrostatic mediators to modulate the reactants delivery modes for the Ag catalytic sites. In addition, the secondary coordination sphere of active site can also be modified in MOF membrane to stabilize the key reaction intermediates for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ to  CO  (Fig. 11(e)).  The  reported  preparation  strategy  is general  and  applicable  to  other  different  types  of  metal nanoparticles [86]. As for the $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{NU-1000~MOF}$ electrocatalyst, the pre-loading of $\mathrm{{Cu(II)}}$ sites in NU-1000 film was conducted via solvothermal  deposition,  and  each $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ node  of  MOF  can immobilize about $4.0\mathrm{Cu}$ . The electroactive Cu nanoparticles near the  MOF/FTO  interface  were  prepared  through  applying  a negative potential. The Cu@NU-1000 MOF exhibited a Faradaic efficiency  of $31\%$ at $-0.82\mathrm{~V~}$ vs.  RHE  with  formate  as  reaction product. The crystallinity and morphology of $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{NU-1000}$ also were  stable  after  a  long-term  electrocatalysis  test.  The  diameter and  spatial  effects  of $\mathrm{Cu}$ nanostructure@MOF  showed  great potential manipulation on $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ remains a great challenge [87]. Recently, researchers found that the sizes of Cu nanoparticles with ${\sim}1.5$ and ${\sim}0.9\ \mathrm{nm}$ can be well manipulated by the pore diameters modulation  of  MOFs.  The  synthesis  method  can  inspire  the structure−property relationship in $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{MOF}$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ [88]. The Cu  cluster@HKUST-1  was  prepared  through  changing  paddlewheel  Cu  dimer  in  MOF  from  symmetric  to  asymmetric  motif using  a  simple  thermal  treatment.  The  surface  coordination number of Cu clusters was judiciously controlled thus leading to a promoted  catalytic  selectivity,  activity  as  well  as  efficiency  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}.$ The Faradaic efficiency (FE) of $\mathrm{C_{2}H_{4}}$ production reached a  record $45\%$ for  MOF-tuned  Cu  cluster  catalysts  [89].  The organic  film  such  as  pyridinium-based  additives  was  also confirmed to greatly promote the selectivity of $\mathrm{Ag}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}$ up to ${>}99\%$ Faradaic  efficiencies  for  electrocatalytic ${\mathrm{CO}}_{2}{\mathrm{RR}},$ which  is also  shedding  light  on  the  design  of  metal@MOF $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ electrocatalysts [90].
## 4.2.2    Metal oxide@MOFs
## 4.2.3    POM@MOFs
Polyoxometalate-metalloporphyrin organic frameworks $\left({\mathrm{POM}}{\textcircled{\omega}}{\mathrm{MOFs}}\right)$ were  developed  by  integrating  cluster  and metalloporphyrin (Fig. 13) [21]. The polyoxometalate acted as an electron provider. The inherent macrocycle conjugated $\pi$ -electron system in metalloporphyrin can promote the electron conductivity. The composite exhibited superior Faradaic efficiency of $>94\%$ for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO conversion over a wide potential range from $-0.8$ to $-1.0\mathrm{V}$ . The turnover frequency is as high as $^{1,656\mathrm{h^{-1}}}$ and the material can stabilize for $>36\mathrm{h}$ .
## 4.2.4    Nanocarbon@MOFs
A unique 3D anionic In-based MOF V11 with good solvent, $\mathrm{pH},$ and  thermal  stability  was  prepared  (Fig. 14)  [92].  In  addition, methylene blue molecules were introduced into the pores of V11 and  the  carbon  nanoparticles  were  further  prepared in  situ by calcination  to  form  carbon  nanostructure $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{V}}\boldsymbol{1}\boldsymbol{1}$ catalysts. Compared  with  the  low $76.0\%$ $\mathrm{FE_{HCOO}}^{}$ at $-0.84\mathrm{~V~}$ of  pristine MOF  V11,  the  new  carbon  nanostructure $\boldsymbol{\mathcal{Q}}\boldsymbol{\mathrm{V}}\boldsymbol{1}{1}$ showed  a significantly enhanced activity with a maximum $\mathrm{FE_{HCOO}}^{}$ of $90.1\%$ at $-0.84\mathrm{V}$ . The experimental results suggest that methylene bluederived carbon particles can be used as promoters to improve the electrical conductivity of the material, expose more active sites and facilitate  charge/mass  transfer  during  the  electrocatalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction. Importantly, the strategy demonstrated a good generality for other classical MOFs and may bring new inspiration for the development of efficient MOFs-based catalysts for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}.$
## 4.2.5    Metallocene@MOFs
Metallocene $(\mathrm{Fe/Co/Ni/Ti/Zr/HfCp_{2},C p}$ means cyclopentadienyl) is featured with high stability and notable aromaticity. Especially, the d-orbits in central metal can trigger a big $\pi$ -electron system of cyclopentadienyl  implying  that  the  metallocene  can  serve  as electron-donating or carrying units. In recent years, metallocene has  attracted  great  interest  from  nanostructure  synthesis  to catalytic  applications  [93–97].  In  order  to  overcome  the constraints  of  low  electron  conductivity  and  poor  electrondonating  capability  of  MOFs  for  electrochemical $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction reaction $(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR})$ ,  metallocene $\mathrm{CoCp}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}545{-}\mathrm{Co}$ was prepared  (Fig. 15).  The  resultant  catalyst  exhibited  outstanding $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ electrocatalysis activity with a high $\mathrm{FE}_{\mathrm{CO}}$ of $97\%$ at $-0.7\mathrm{V}$ The encapsulated metallocene can induce the formation of strong binding-interaction with metalloporphyrin in MOF-545-Co, and the  role  of  metallocene  as  electron  donator  and  carrier  for promoting the continuity of electron transfer in MOF frameworks can  be  enhanced.  Thus  adsorption  energy  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ on  the $\mathrm{CoCp}_{2}@\mathrm{MOF}{-}545{-}\mathrm{Co}$ was  largely  reduced  leading  to  the enhanced $\mathrm{CO}_{2}\mathrm{RR}$ activity [93].
## 4.3    Photoelectrocatalysis
It is well known that porphyrin component in chlorophyll is the photosensitive core in the photosynthetic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. A series of photo-coupled electrocatalysts of Au, Cu, and $\scriptstyle\mathrm{Co}$ single atom loaded  hollow $\mathrm{Zr}$ -porphyrinic-MOF $\mathrm{\langleHNTM-Au/Cu/Co-SA\rangle}$ were prepared to simulate the structure of chlorophyll (Figs. 16(a) and 16(b)).  The  Au  single  atom  uniformly  immobilized  into
HNTM-Au-SA  while  the  pristine  nanostructure  of  HNTM-AuSA  was  still  well  retained.  Without  the  porphyrinic  anchoring sites, Au nanoparticles with sizes ranging from 5 to $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ were formed. Compared with Au nanoparticles sample, HNTM-Au-SA sample presented a high $94.2\%$ CO Faradaic efficiency at $-0.9{\mathrm{~V~}}$ and a notable turnover frequency of $37,069~\mathrm{h^{-1}}$ at $-1.1\mathrm{~V~}$ . When the  light  illumination  was  introduced,  the  overpotentials  of HNTM-Au-SA,  HNTM-Cu-SA,  and  HNTM-Co-SA  exhibited positive  shifts  of  130,  100,  and $20,\ \mathrm{mV}$ ,  respectively,  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, which is well correlated with their energy gap values (Figs. 16(c) and 16(d)). The findings strongly verified that both the size and the electron transfer pathway on metal center can be well manipulated by the porphyrin component in MOF [16, 75]. The design  of  p-type  photocathode  is  very  important  for photoelectrochemical  (PEC)  conversion  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ into  chemical fuels  and  feedstocks  to  address  the  energy  and  environmental challenges.  The  p-type $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ semiconductor  was  encapsulated into $\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}$ -MOF $\mathrm{'Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}\ @\mathrm{MOF})$ in  order  to  overcome  the photocorrosion  problem  of $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ .  Encouragingly,  the  prepared $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}\mathrm{O@MOF}$ presented  a  notably  promoted  activity  compared with the single $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ in PEC conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to CO. Ultrafast spectroscopy  confirmed  the  occurrence  of  greatly  enhanced charge separation and transfer at the interface between $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ and MOF. Moreover, The MOF coating can effectively promote both the  activity  and  long-term  stability  of $\mathrm{{Cu_{2}O}}$ through  preventing photocorrosion and offering more active sites for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ activation [98].
## 4.4    Thermal catalysis
Catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation into high-valued fuels and platform chemicals has great potential in constructing a sustainable carbon cycle  [99, 100].  Owing  to  the  abundant  tenability  in  secondary building  units  of  metal  clusters  and  organic  linkers,  MOF  has received  great  popularity  as  a  tunable  catalyst  and/or  support platforms for heterogeneous catalysis. The $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation of MOFs-based catalysts for producing $\mathrm{CH}_{4},$ $\mathrm{J}_{4},\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH},$ , HCOOH, and alkyl  formates  has  been  widely  investigated  in  recent  years. Therein, the manipulation of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{C-C}$ bond formation and the $\mathrm{C-O}$ bond  retaining  is  scientifically  challenging,  which  is  the  major hurdle in achieving a high selectivity to oxygenates, especially $\mathrm{C}_{2+}$ products.
## 4.4.1    Metal@MOFs
HKUST-1  film  without  Cu  nanoparticles  exhibited  no  catalytic activity  for  MeOH  oxidation  reaction.  The  catalytic  activity  for methanol oxidation was found to be highly depending on the sizes of the copper nanoparticles, in which $4{\mathrm{-}}6\ \mathrm{nm}$ copper clusters can selectively  generate  formaldehyde  molecules  and  the $8\mathrm{-}12\ \mathrm{nm}$ copper nanoparticles can produce extra $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ due to the formation of  formate  species.  Moreover,  the  Cu-HKUST-1  interface  sites have also been confirmed to stabilize the MeOH molecules and inhibit aggregation during the continuous oxidative process [105]. Based on the full characterizations, $\mathrm{Cu}$ nanoparticles are regarded as active sites for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation in the form of $\mathrm{{Cu-H}}$ species. The process of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to methanol occurs at $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ clusters of UiO-66 by coupling CO species and the spillover H. The reason for performance enhancement in $\mathrm{Cu}@$ amorphous UiO-66 should be attributed to the existence of a larger number of open $\mathrm{zr}$ sites, which can not only promote the stabilization of $\mathrm{Cu}$ nanoparticles, but also enhance $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ dissociation and H spillover [106]. The above researches provide in-depth insights into the understanding about the  roles  of  nanoparticles  and  open  nodes  in  regulating  the formation  of  active  hydrogen  and  intermediate  species, respectively, in the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation reaction for nanostructure@MOF  structures.  Moreover,  a  series  of $\mathrm{Cu}@\upgamma-$ $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3},$ $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{ZIF}{-}8$ , $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{MIL-}100$ and $\mathrm{Cu}@\mathrm{UiO}–66$ composites were  compared  in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  to  methanol  reaction  to study  the  relationship  between  charge  transfer  dependence  and catalytic  activity.  By  contrast,  UiO-66  is  verified  to  be  the  best support for $\mathrm{Cu},$ and the defects of $\mathrm{Zr}_{6}$ -clusters in UiO-66 showed invisible  effects  on  the $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  performance. Compared  with $\mathrm{Cu}@\gamma{-}\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ reference,  the $\operatorname{Cu@}Z\mathbf{r}$ -UiO-66 produced a 70 times higher methanol generation rate. When $\mathrm{Zr^{4+}}$ was replaced with $\mathrm{Hf^{\mu_{+}}}$ , Cu@Hf-UiO-66 can further triple for the methanol  production  rate.  The  charge  transfer  from  Cu nanoparticles to UiO-66 support was suggested to be responsible for the activity enhancements of Cu [18, 107, 108].
## 4.4.2    Metal oxide@MOFs
Although  metal  nanostructure@MOFs  exhibited  attractive $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation catalytic activities, non-noble metal oxide@MOFs have also been studied. The $\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}@\mathrm{two}$ types of ZIF-8 and MIL53(Al) were prepared for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation. The MOFs structures are stable under the reaction conditions of $3\mathrm{MPa}$ and 573  K.  Owing  to  the  great $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ adsorption  property, ${\mathrm{Fe}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}@{\mathrm{MOFs}}$ showed  superior  catalytic  activity  compared  with traditional ${\bf g}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm O}_{3}$ support. Interestingly, ZIF-8 and MIL-53(Al) produced  different  effects  on  the  catalytic  activity  and  the selectivity.  The  acidity  of  MIL-53(Al)  leaded  to  the  activity enhancement  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation  and  the  extremely  high selectivity to alkanes. By contrast, ZIF-8 has the superior ability for hydrogen storage and the potential rich $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ in the pores of MOF, which should promote the secondary hydrogenation reaction. As a result, the ZIF-8 showed a high selectivity towards light olefins. The  finding  implied  that  the  properties  of  inner  metal  oxide particles  can  be  well  modulated  by  different  types  of  MOFs supports [109]. The catalytic conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ via hydrogenation pathway is a very important way to produce clean methanol fuel. Inspired by the crucial roles of $\mathrm{{Cu/ZnO}}$ and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ interfaces in industrially  used  ternary $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}/\mathrm{Al}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ catalyst  for  methanol production, $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}$ nanoparticles anchored UiO-bpy MOF was prepared,  in  which  the  strong  metal-support  interaction  (SMSI) between  metal-oxo  clusters  and $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}$ nanoparticles  and  the confinement effect by the rich porosity of MOF were reported to be responsible for the enhanced catalytic activity and selectivity of $\mathrm{Cu}/\mathrm{ZnO}_{x}$ @UiO-bpy MOF (Fig. 18) [25].
## 4.4.3    Cluster@MOFs
In  addition  to  metal  or  metal  oxide  nanoparticles,  highly  active sub-nanometer metal clusters were also successfully encapsulated into  MOFs  frameworks  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation. Supporting  sub-nanometer  metal  clusters  on  the  highly  porous MOF  matrix  has  been  verified  to  be  an  effective  method  to overcome the synthesis and stabilization challenges. Dinuclear $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{}0}$ clusters@MOFs catalyst with well-controlled atomicity, electronics and  structure  was  prepared  through  a  thioether  functionalized MOF channels (Fig. 19). The formation of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{}0}$ dimers should go through  mutual  proximity  of  two  neighboring $\mathrm{Pt}^{2+}{-}S$ metals anchored  at  flexible  thio-ether  groups  inside  the  pores  of  MOF during $\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}$ reduction process. X-ray absorption fine structure spectrum  (EXAFS)  of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\mathrm{0}}$ clusters@MOFs  indicated  a  2.65  Å distance  between $\mathrm{Pt^{\circ}-P t^{\circ}}$ . $\mathrm{Pt}_{0}^{2}$ dimers  are  also  suggested  to  own
Pt···O  distances  of  2.79  and $2.80\mathrm{~\AA~}$ through  weakly  interacting with  oxamate  moieties.  As  for  the  hydrogenation  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methane reaction, activation energy of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{}0}$ clusters@MOFs is 35 $\mathrm{{KJ}\cdot\mathrm{{mol}^{-1}}}$ , which is much lower than 65 KJ·mol–1. Therefore, the $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{}0}$ clusters@MOFs can allow the hydrogenation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to methane reaction to occur at low temperature $(<140^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The multi-gram scale synthesis of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{0}0}$ clusters@MOFs as well as the high loading amount $(8~\mathrm{wt.\%})$ of $\mathrm{Pt}_{2}^{\phantom{0}0}$ is also very attractive for other catalytic methodology [110].
The $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{I}}\ @$ $\mathrm{Zr}_{12}{\mathrm{-MOF}}$ was  prepared  to  achieve  the  selective conversion of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to ethanol through generating C-C bond while maintaining  C-O  bond  intact.  The  active $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites  were modulated by elaborately utilizing the secondary building units of $\mathrm{Zr_{12}\mathrm{-}M O F}$ (Fig. 20).  The  introduction  of  extra  alkali  cations  can not only finely tune the local structure of catalytic centres but also function  an  interesting  promoters  to  obtain  a  notable  ethanol selectivity as high as $599\%$ and a high turnover number of 4,080 (operating conditions: supercritical $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ 30 MPa $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ 5 MPa $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ . Specially, alkali-metal promoters can generate an electronrich local environment for $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}^{\mathrm{{I}}}$ sites to promote $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ activation as well as the formyl intermediate stabilization. The work not only suggested the novel strategy for bimetallic centres preparation but also the great promise of sub-nanometer active sites for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation [32].
## 4.4.4    Molecular Complexe@MOFs
Ir  polypyridine@UiO-type  MOFs  was  developed  for $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formate. The Ir complex was incorporated into UiO-type MOFs in the form of isolated Ir site by utilizing proper ligands  and  -OH  design  on  the  6-position.  Interestingly,  the catalysis was well achieved by replacing Ir polypyridine@UiO-type MOFs  in  a  Soxhlet-type  reflux-condensing  system  to  effectively constructing  a  dynamic  tri-phasic  interface  of $\mathrm{CO_{2}/H_{2}\mathrm{-}H_{2}O\mathrm{-}}$ catalyst  (Fig. 21(a)).  Then  the  contact  among  the  reactants  was maximized  by  the  dynamic  gas/liquid/solid  interface.  The produced  formic  acid  was  removed  to  the  refluxing  flask  by siphoning  thus  facilitating  the  positive  movement  of  catalytic reactions $(\mathrm{H}_{2}(\mathrm{g)\+\CO}_{2}(\mathrm{g)\=\HCO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ (aq)).  A  high  turnover frequency of about $410\mathrm{{h^{-1}}}$ was achieved in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formate under the operating conditions of atmospheric pressure and $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 21(b)). Then the contact among the reactants was maximized  by  the  dynamic  gas/liquid/solid  interface.  The produced  formic  acid  was  removed  to  the  refluxing  flask  by siphoning  thus  facilitating  the  positive  movement  of  catalytic reactions $\mathrm{(H}_{2}(\mathrm{g)\+\CO}_{2}(\mathrm{g)\=\HCO}_{2}\mathrm{H}$ (aq)).  A  high  turnover frequency of about $410\mathrm{{h^{-1}}}$ was achieved in $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ hydrogenation to formate under the operating conditions of atmospheric pressure and $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. 21(b)).  It  is  well-known  that  the $\mathrm{H}/\mathrm{D}$ kinetic isotope  effects  (KIEs)  measurements  have  not  been  well implemented  in  in  homogeneous  systems  due  to  the  masking effect of base-catalyzed H/D exchange. In this work, the notable KIEs  of  Ir  polypyridine@UiO-type  MOFs  and  the  DFT calculations  strongly  verified  that  the  rate-determining  step  was promoted  through  the  intensive  proton-hydride  transfer  via quantum  tunneling  (Fig. 21(c)).  The  work  also  suggests  that  the immobilization  of  molecular  complexes  into  MOFs  is  a  highly effective  strategy  to  reveal  the  obscure  reaction  mechanism research in homogeneous systems [111].
## 5 Elucidation of structure-activity relationship
## 6    Conclusions and perspectives
The review summarizes the recent advances of nanostructure@MOFs in catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction on the basis of photocatalysis, electrocatalysis, photoelectrocatalysis, and thermal catalysis.  Despite  the  fact  that  the  great  progress  of  emerging nanostructure@MOFs have been witnessed, this research field is still  at  the  early  stage.  According  to  the  functions  of  MOFs  in nanostructure@MOFs  catalysts,  we  believe  that  the  following challenges  are  urgently  needed  to  be  addressed.  Firstly,  the synthesis  of  confined  low-dimension  nanostructures  with  welldefined size, structure and spatial arrangement in MOFs is highly desired. Secondly, MOFs are usually serving as a front-loaded $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capturing  materials  to  concentrate/enrich $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ molecules  for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction.  Then  the  pore  structure  and microenvironment  (such  as  hydrophobicity,  node  and  ligand) should  be  directionally  modulated  to  achieve $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ -specific adsorption  from  other  competing  gas  molecules.  The  dynamic adsorption and desorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ in MOFs are also attractive to investigate  the  mass  transport  and  reaction  balance  in nanostructure@MOFs  catalysts.  Thirdly,  in-depth  understanding of the effects of interfacial structure and microenvironment of the active  sites  on  the  regulation  of  adsorption  and  desorption energies  of  reaction  molecules/intermediates/products  is  an important  prerequisite  for  revealing  the  structure-activity relationship in nanostructure@MOFs catalysts, which is also very urgent  for  the  development  of  next-generation  nanostructure $@$ MOFs  catalysts.  The  reaction  pathway/mechanism  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction is featured with multi-step proton and electron transfer, which involves various reaction intermediates related to multiple products.  To  decouple  the  complex  process  of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ reduction, integrating  advanced operando characterization  techniques  with free energy calculations especially under real operating conditions is highly desirable. Last but not the least, more endeavors are still highly required to systematically promote the stability, selectivity, and activity of nanostructure@MOFs for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. In  addition,  the  integration  of  two  or  all  of  the  three  types  of photo/electro/thermal  catalysis  is  emerging  as  a  synergic enhancement  system  for  catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion.  All  in  all, nanostructure@MOFs  materials  are  already  presenting  great promise as next-generation systems for catalytic $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion. The  new  perspectives  will  stimulate  the  rapid  development  of nanostructure@MOFs materials to largely fulfill the requirements for practical catalytic applications in the near future.
